n, checking on the river seems like the low-risk/high-reward strategy.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The game has been extremely passive with the opponent checking on every street (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, and River). This indicates a weak range, likely consisting of missed high cards or weak pairs. You hold King-high (5d, Ks) on a board of 7d, 6c, 9s, 3s, 2h. While King-high is not a strong made hand, it often has enough showdown value to win against a missed draw or lower high cards (like Q-high or J-high) in a limped pot.\n\nBetting here has negative value for two reasons: \n1.  **Fold Equity:** Better hands (like any pair or Ace-high) will often call a river bet in this scenario, meaning you lose chips.\n2.  **Value:** Worse hands (like Q-high) will almost certainly fold, so you gain nothing.\n\nSince you are currently in the lead and the pot is small, the best strategy is to minimize variance and realize your equity for free.\n\nReason: Checking is the safest option. You have some showdown value with King-high, and betting is unlikely to cause better hands to fold or worse hands to call. Checking back guarantees you see the showdown without risking additional chips.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
